# repv-servers.csv - the alias table: what each short name in an inventory
#                       means. ONE table for the whole fleet.
#
# Name it whatever suits the job and pass it with --servers. One table can serve
# every job on a host, or each job can have its own:
#
#     cp repv-servers.csv.example /root/couchdb-repv/repv-servers.csv
#
# This file holds NO credentials, and that is the point of it being separate
# from the --env file. It is meant to be committed and reviewed: when a backup
# turns out to be missing, "which box was bak1 in August?" has to be answerable
# from git, and it cannot be if the topology lives in a 0600 file nobody reads.
#
# The header row is authoritative - column order is free, but a missing column
# is a startup error rather than a silently shifted field. Blank lines and #
# lines are ignored. There is no quoting: neither an alias nor these URLs can
# contain a comma.
#
#   alias   what an inventory row writes in its src-svr / targ-svr / rep-svr
#           columns. Letters, digits, . _ - only. It can never contain '://',
#           which is what lets an inventory tell an alias from a full URL.
#   url     scheme://host[:port], no path, NO credentials.
#
# Name aliases after the BOX, not the role. The same machine is the targ-svr of
# one row and the rep-svr of another - SRC_/TARG_/REP_ prefixes would force it
# to appear twice under two names, and the two would drift apart.
#
# Two aliases may point at one box if you want both names; the first one listed
# is the one used in log lines. A duplicate alias is an error, not last-wins.
#
# Credentials come from the --env file, looked up by the hostname below:
# 192.168.66.5 here wants 192.168.66.5_USER and 192.168.66.5_PASS there. If two
# CouchDBs share a box on different ports, a port-qualified key
# (192.168.66.5:6984_USER) takes precedence over the bare hostname.
#
# A url here carrying credentials is REFUSED, not warned about. The env file is
# the only source: a second one means two places to look when a password stops
# working, and puts a secret in the one config file meant to be committed.
#
alias,url

# the LAN backup nodes
bak1,http://192.168.66.5:5984
bak2,http://192.168.66.11:5984

# the CloudVendor production node
cloud,http://203.0.113.10:5984

# the test VMs
test0,http://192.168.66.30:5984
test1,http://192.168.66.31:5984
